 ],
 "caption": "Whistler's Mother only exists because a model didn't show up.\n\nLondon, 1871: James McNeill Whistler was waiting for a 15-year-old girl to pose for another painting, Annabel Lee. She never came. So he asked his mother, Anna, then 67. Her letter remembers his words: \"Mother I want you to stand for me!\" She tried, but standing was too tiring, so he painted her sitting down.\n\nHe didn't even want it seen as a portrait. He called it Arrangement in Grey and Black, and later wrote: \"what can or ought the public to care about the identity of the portrait?\" He even pawned it; the French state bought it in 1891.\n\nThen, in 1934, the US put her on a 3-cent Mother's Day stamp, cropped, with a vase of flowers added: \"In memory and in honor of the mothers of America.\" The stand-in had become America's mother.\n\n🖤 Would you have guessed she was a last-minute replacement?",
 "caption_short": "She's only in this painting because a model didn't show up. Whistler's Mother was a stand-in. 🖤",
